filiation": "University of Washington, Seattle, Washington, USA."}]}}, {"fullName": "Piha-Paul SA", "lastName": "Piha-Paul", "firstName": "Sarina A", "initials": "SA", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-9455-1660"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "University of Texas MD Anderson Cancer Center, Houston, Texas, USA."}]}}, {"fullName": "Yearley J", "lastName": "Yearley", "firstName": "Jennifer", "initials": "J", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Merck & Co. Inc., Kenilworth, New Jersey, USA."}]}}, {"fullName": "Seiwert TY", "lastName": "Seiwert", "firstName": "Tanguy Y", "initials": "TY", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "University of Chicago, Chicago, Illinois, USA."}]}}, {"fullName": "Ribas A", "lastName": "Ribas", "firstName": "Antoni", "initials": "A", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0003-3669-8458"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "UCLA, Los Angeles, California, USA."}]}}, {"fullName": "McClanahan TK", "lastName": "McClanahan", "firstName": "Terrill K", "initials": "TK", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Merck & Co. Inc., Kenilworth, New Jersey, USA."}]}}]}, "authorIdList": {"authorId": [{"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-9455-1660"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0003-3669-8458"}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["altmetrics", "recommended_reviews", "supporting_data"]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "8", "volume": "127", "journalIssueId": 2581636, "dateOfPublication": "2017 Aug", "monthOfPublication": 8, "yearOfPublication": 2017, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "J Clin Invest", "title": "The Journal of clinical investigation", "essn": "1558-8238", "issn": "0021-9738", "nlmid": "7802877", "isoabbreviation": "J Clin Invest"}, "printPublicationDate": "2017-08-01"}, "pubYear": "2017", "pageInfo": "2930-2940", "abstractText": "Programmed death-1-directed (PD-1-directed) immune checkpoint blockade results in durable antitumor activity in many advanced malignancies. Recent studies suggest that IFN-γ is a critical driver of programmed death ligand-1 (PD-L1) expression in cancer and host cells, and baseline intratumoral T cell infiltration may improve response likelihood to anti-PD-1 therapies, including pembrolizumab. However, whether quantifying T cell-inflamed microenvironment is a useful pan-tumor determinant of PD-1-directed therapy response has not been rigorously evaluated. Here, we analyzed gene expression profiles (GEPs) using RNA from baseline tumor samples of pembrolizumab-treated patients. We identified immune-related signatures correlating with clinical benefit using a learn-and-confirm paradigm based on data from different clinical studies of pembrolizumab, starting with a small pilot of 19 melanoma patients and eventually defining a pan-tumor T cell-inflamed GEP in 220 patients with 9 cancers. Predictive value was independently confirmed and compared with that of PD-L1 immunohistochemistry in 96 patients with head and neck squamous cell carcinoma. The T cell-inflamed GEP contained IFN-γ-responsive genes related to antigen presentation, chemokine expression, cytotoxic activity, and adaptive immune resistance, and these features were necessary, but not always sufficient, for clinical benefit. The T cell-inflamed GEP has been developed into a clinical-grade assay that is currently being evaluated in ongoing pembrolizumab trials.", "affiliation": "Merck & Co. Inc., Kenilworth, New Jersey, USA.", "publicationStatus": "ppublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Print-Electronic", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["research-article", "Clinical Trial, Phase I", "Randomized Controlled Trial", "Journal Article"]}, "meshHeadingList": {"meshHeading": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Immune System"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Humans"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Me